# Unity C# formatting

Based on [Unity's formatting article](https://unity.com/how-to/formatting-best-practices-c-scripting-unity). Apply rules uniformly; resolve disagreements from this guide. IDE enforcement: root `.editorconfig`.

## Properties

Properties expose state through **get/set accessors** (often backed by a private field). Use them to encapsulate data, validate input, or convert units — not as a public field shortcut.

| Style | When | Example |
|-------|------|---------|
| Expression-bodied | Single-line **read-only** | `public float Speed => m_speed;` |
| Auto-implemented | No custom backing field logic | `public int Score { get; private set; }` |
| Full accessors | Validation, side effects, or multi-line logic | see below |

- **Read-only exposure**: expression-bodied getter returning a backing field, or `{ get; }` / `{ get; private set; }`.
- **Restrict writes**: `private set` (or `init` when appropriate) instead of public setters you do not need.
- **Everything else**: `{ get; set; }` blocks — not expression-bodied setters. Align closing braces with opening braces on multi-line property bodies.

```csharp
float m_health;

// read-only
public float Health => m_health;

// controlled write
public int Score { get; private set; }

// validation / multi-line
public float HealthClamped
{
    get => m_health;
    set => m_health = Mathf.Max(0f, value);
}
```

## Class organization

**Goal: short, focused classes** (single responsibility — see `unity-clean-code-principles`). If a type needs many unrelated sections, split it before the file becomes a "god class."

Standardize each type with **member sections** in this order (blank line between sections — see Vertical spacing):

1. **Fields** — constants (`k_`), static (`s_`), then instance (`m_`)
2. **Properties**
3. **Events / delegates**
4. **Unity lifecycle** — `Awake`, `OnEnable`, `Start`, `Update`, `FixedUpdate`, `LateUpdate`, `OnDisable`, `OnDestroy`, etc.
5. **Public methods**
6. **Private methods**

**Files:** one `MonoBehaviour` per `.cs` file; **filename = behaviour class name**. Other helper types may live in the same file. Multiple behaviours in one file only if separated by namespaces (unusual — prefer one behaviour per file). Naming details: `unity-csharp-naming`.

```csharp
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    const int k_MaxHp = 100;
    [SerializeField] float m_speed;

    public float Speed => m_speed;
    public event Action Died;

    void Awake() { … }
    void OnEnable() { … }
    void Update() { … }
    void OnDisable() { … }

    public void TakeDamage(int amount) { … }
    void RefreshUI() { … }
}
```

## Serialization

Unity serializes object state for scenes, prefabs, and assets. It differs from typical .NET serialization — follow the [Script Serialization](https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/script-Serialization.html) manual for supported types.

- **Inspector fields**: `public` **or** `[SerializeField]` on private/protected fields — prefer `[SerializeField]` + `m_` naming (see `unity-csharp-naming`).
- **Not serialized**: `static`, `const`, and `readonly` fields.
- **`[Range(min, max)]`**: clamp numeric Inspector values; shows as a slider.
- **Inspector groups**: `[Serializable]` class/struct with public fields, referenced from a behaviour — collapsible sections in the Inspector.

```csharp
[SerializeField] float m_speed;
[SerializeField, Range(0f, 100f)] float m_health;

[Serializable]
public struct CombatStats
{
    public float attack;
    public float defense;
}

[SerializeField] CombatStats m_stats;
```

## Braces and indentation

Pick **one** brace style for the whole team — this project uses **Allman** (opening `{` on its own line), per Microsoft / Unity examples. Do not mix **K&R** (brace on same line as header).

- **4 spaces**, spaces not tabs (convert tabs in IDE settings).
- **Never omit braces**, even for single-line `if`/`for`/`while` — easier breakpoints and safe edits when adding lines later.
- **Nested blocks**: keep braces on multiline nests even when C# allows omission — avoids dangling-else confusion.
- **`switch`**: indent `case` bodies; include `default` even when enum cases look exhaustive (guards unexpected values).

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — K&R and no braces
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    DoThing(i);

// ✅ GOOD — Allman + braces
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    DoThing(i);
}

switch (state)
{
    case GameState.Playing:
        Tick();
        break;
    default:
        break;
}
```

## Horizontal spacing

Whitespace separates tokens on a line — use it consistently so density stays readable. Prefer **string interpolation** over concatenation or `string.Format`.

| Rule | Do | Don't |
|------|-----|-------|
| Commas | `Foo(a, b, c)` | `Foo(a,b,c)` |
| Method call | `DoThing(arg)` | `DoThing (arg)` |
| After `(` in call | no space before args | `Foo( arg )` |
| Brackets | `array[0]` | `array[ 0 ]` when avoidable |
| Flow keywords | `if (x == 0)` | `if(x==0)` |
| Comparisons | `a == b`, `i < n` | `a==b` |
| Line width | break near **80–120** chars | one endless line |

- **Indentation** expresses hierarchy (see Braces) — do not rely on column-aligning variable names across declarations; pairing type and name gets harder.
- **Column alignment** is optional only for dense structs, flag blocks, or similar — formatters may reflow it.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
Calculate (m_health,m_armor);
if(count>0)Process();
items[ index ];

// ✅ GOOD
Calculate(m_health, m_armor);
if (count > 0)
{
    Process();
}
items[index];
```

## Vertical spacing and regions

Use blank lines to show structure — keep related code together, separate unrelated blocks.

- **Group related methods** so readers do not jump around the file (`Tick`, `FixedTick`, `LateTick` adjacent).
- **Separate major sections** with one or two blank lines when it helps (follow **Class organization** order):
  - fields → properties → events → lifecycle → methods
  - nested types / interfaces in the same file
  - long `if` / `else` chains (sparingly)
- Use vertical space **minimally** — only where it aids scanning; document team habits if you standardize double blanks anywhere.

### `#region`

`#region` collapses hunks in the IDE but hides structure. **Prefer smaller classes** over regions when a file grows. If the team allows regions, use them rarely and name them clearly; many teams treat regions as a smell — this project **defaults to no `#region`**.

```csharp
[SerializeField] float m_speed;
[SerializeField] float m_health;

void Update()
{
    TickMovement();
    TickCombat();
}

void TickMovement() { … }
void TickCombat() { … }
```
